
% WARNING: PROPRIETARY; MOVE this and opposite to separate test too!
the templates are:
    we will make *a payment* ; 
        opposite: we will not make *a payment*.
    we will pay *an amount*;
        opposite: we will not pay *an amount*.
    we will cover *a cost*;
        opposite: we will not cover *a cost* .
    
    % templates that are preposisional or subsidiary; for binary predicates, and must start with an argument
    % allows omitting first argument when chaining to a type-compatible last arg of the previous condition
    *a payment* under *a policy*; 
        prepositional.  
    *a payment* in respect of *a claim*; 
        prepositional.
    *a payment* for *an amount*;
        prepositional.
    *an amount* for all relevant claims or losses covered under *a section* of *a policy* during *a period*.
    *an amount* in respect of *a claim*;
        prepositional.
    *a claim* against *a person*;
        prepositional.
    *a cost* is in respect of *a damage*;
        prepositional.
    *a damage* is caused by *a cause*;
        prepositional.

    % fictional helpers:
    our company loves *an incident*.
    our company recognizes *a claim*.
    our company can afford *an amount*.
    customer is happy with *an incident*;
        opposite: customer is unhappy with *an incident*.
    customer becomes richer with *a claim*.

    our policy is *a policy*;  
        defines global this policy; defines global your policy.   
        % p(this policy) <-> our policy is X and p(X)

the ontology is:
    wear and tear or negligence is a cause.
    burst pipe is a cause.

the knowledge base big_conclusions includes:

% Form 1
we will not make a payment under this policy in respect of an incident unless
    our company loves the incident.

customer is happy with an incident if 
    we will make a payment under this policy in respect of the incident.

% Form 2
we will make a payment under a policy in respect of a claim if 
    our company recognizes the claim.

we will make a payment under a policy in respect of a claim if 
    our company recognizes the claim and
    it is not the case that
        we will not make the payment under this policy in respect of the claim.

% Form 3
we will pay an amount in respect of a claim only if 
    our company recognizes the claim.

customer becomes richer with a claim if
    we will pay an amount in respect of the claim.

% Form 4
we will make a payment for an amount 
    for all relevant claims or losses covered under a section of your policy during a period 
    only if
        our company can afford the amount.

customer is happy with an incident if 
    we will make a payment for an amount for all relevant claims or losses covered under a section of your policy during a period.

% Form 5
we will not make a payment under a policy unless 
    the payment in respect of a claim and % could introduce alternate template "*a payment* is in respect of *a claim*" and an additional rule
    our company recognizes the claim.

customer is unhappy with an incident if 
    we will not make a payment under this policy.

% Form 6
we will make a payment under a policy in respect of a claim against a person if 
    our company recognizes the claim.

customer becomes richer with a claim if 
    we will make a payment under a policy in respect of the claim against a person.


% Form 7
we will cover a cost in respect of a damage caused by a burst pipe if
    it is not the case that
        the damage is caused by wear and tear or negligence.


% --- Test scenarios exercising the prepositional-chaining feature ---

% Form 2 (positive head): we_will_make from "our company recognizes" + chained
% facts under/2 and in_respect_of/2. We deliberately do NOT set "our policy is"
% so Form 1's we_will_not_make cannot fire and conflict with the conclusion.
scenario form2_facts is:
    our company recognizes claim_C.
    payment_P under policy_A.
    payment_P in respect of claim_C.
    our company loves claim_C.
    q_make_payment expects answers ["we will make payment_P"].

% Form 6: same as Form 2 plus a "claim against person" fact, exercising the
% second-level chain (*a claim* against *a person* on top of *a payment* in
% respect of *a claim*).
scenario form6_facts is:
    our company recognizes claim_C.
    payment_P under policy_A.
    payment_P in respect of claim_C.
    claim_C against alice.
    our company loves claim_C.
    q_richer_claim expects answers ["customer becomes richer with claim_C"].

% Form 1 (negative head via opposite + chain): we_will_not_make is derived when
% our company does NOT love the incident.
scenario form1_facts is:
    our policy is policy_A.
    payment_P under policy_A.
    payment_P in respect of incident_I.
    q_no_make expects answers ["we will not make payment_P"].

% Form 7: we_will_cover via the "is in respect of" / "is caused by" chain,
% where the chain matches even though the user omits the leading copula "is".
scenario form7_facts is:
    cost_X is in respect of damage_D.
    damage_D is caused by burst pipe.
    q_cover_cost expects answers ["we will cover cost_X"].


% --- Queries used by the scenarios above ---

query q_make_payment is:
    we will make which payment.

query q_richer_claim is:
    customer becomes richer with which claim.

query q_no_make is:
    we will not make which payment.

query q_cover_cost is:
    we will cover which cost.

